[1mStack[0m
    dup          x -- x x                           duplicate top of stack
    drop         x --                               drop the top stack element
    swap         a b -- b a                         swap top two elements
    over         a b -- a b a                       copy second item to top
    nip          a b -- b                           drop second-from-top element (keep top)
    tuck         a b -- b a b                       duplicate second item and tuck below top
    roll         ... n -- ...                       move the item at depth n to the top (1 roll = swap, 2 roll = rot)
    pick         ... n -- ... v                     copy the item at depth n to the top (0 pick = dup)
    depth        -- n                               number of items on the stack
    clst         ... --                             clear the stack
    savestack    --                                 save the whole stack to stack_backup.dat in the config directory
    loadstack    -- ...                             push the stack saved by savestack


[1mwords[0m
A word is a command you define: : name body ;  e.g.  : sq dup * ;  then  5 sq  gives 25.
Words run like built-in commands, can call other words and themselves, and are what the
numerical methods take as functions (@sq pushes a token for it). Inside a word:
  flag if ... else ... then      begin ... flag until      begin ... flag while ... repeat
  limit start do ... loop        i (loop index)  j        n +loop
listwords shows yours, "name" delword deletes one, clrwords all; savewords writes them
to ~/.config/mm41/user_words.txt, which loads at startup. A word is one line; put a
family of them in a word module instead (help modules). Manual: chapter Words.


[1msyntax[0m
Tokens are separated by spaces; numbers go on the stack, commands take their arguments
from it (3 4 + is 7). Numbers 3 -1.2e-4 .5, decimals 0.1d, complex (1,2), strings "text",
matrices [rows cols $ a11 a12 ...] or [rows, cols, "file"]. Comments start with \ or #.
The stack prints after every line, [0] at the bottom; undo restores the previous stack,
q quits. help <name> describes any command. Manual: chapter Entering data.


commands mentioning "holiday":
    workdays     d1 d2 -- n                         workdays from d1 to d2, both inclusive (Excel NETWORKDAYS): Mondays to Fridays not in the holiday calendar; negative if d2 < d1
    isworkday    date -- flag                       1 if the date is a Monday to Friday and not a holiday
    holidays     name -- H                          the holiday calendar as a vector of YYYYMMDD: the file holidays/name.txt in the config directory, or the built-in rules "us" (federal, observed) and "si" (Slovenia) for the years around today
    yholidays    year name -- H                     the built-in rules for one year
    sethol       H --                               install a vector of dates YYYYMMDD as the holiday calendar for this session
    clrhol       --                                 remove the holiday calendar (weekends only) and forget it in config.txt
    hol          -- H                               the installed holiday calendar as a vector


